    "narrative": "For today's matchup, **Lawrence Butler** (74.61) stands as our premier offensive anchor. Facing Javier\u2019s high 5.16 xERA, **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a massive 13% boost provided by his dynamic platoon advantage and a favorable spot in the four-hole, positioning him to capitalize on the weak opposing arm. Close behind is **Dylan Beavers** (69.52), who draws a similarly exploitable matchup against Martinez; he is the highest-value start outside the top of the order, surging due to an elite 17% SP skill rating and a generous 16% park factor adjustment. **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) and **JJ Bleday** (68.50) round out our top-tier plays, both thriving thanks to high SP xERA marks from Baz and Bratt, respectively, which effectively inflate their scoring ceilings well into the \"Strong\" territory.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision today was sidelining **Jung Hoo Lee** (50.65). While **Jung Hoo Lee** offers a rare sprint speed profile that typically demands a start in our SB-starved rotation, his dynamic platoon penalty against the left-handed Bennett pushed him just below the threshold of our starting ten. We opted for **Gavin Sheets** (58.58) and **Justin Crawford** (57.02) instead, as the raw scoring efficiency for those two outweighed the incremental stolen base upside **Jung Hoo Lee** provides in this specific platoon matchup.\n\nManagers should monitor the risk surrounding **Cal Raleigh** (55.95), whose score is suppressed by a difficult encounter with Imanaga. While **Cal Raleigh** remains a start due to the depth of the catcher position, his 55.95 rating is borderline; any late lineup shifts or weather developments at the venue could threaten his utility. Similarly, while **Bryce Harper** (66.02) boasts an impressive 0.389 xwOBA, his score is slightly tempered by the presence of a respectable arm in Leahy. We are prioritizing high-ceiling matchups against the likes of Javier and Martinez to maximize our efficiency across the 5x5 categories.",
